<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n32059" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">occallesco</span></b>  (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">obc-</span></b> ), lui, 3, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>v. inch. n.</i> [ob-calleo], <i>to get a thick skin; to grow</i> or <i>become callous.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">latera occallescunt plagis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plaut. As. 2, 4, 13</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Cels. 4, 24</span></i>. —<b>Poet.</b>, of one metamorphosed into a swine: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">os sensi occallescere rostro,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					M. 14, 282</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>to become
			callous</i>, <i>hardened</i>, <i>insensible</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">jam prorsus occallui,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Att. 2, 18, 4</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">longā patientiā occallui,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					Ep. 2, 15, 2</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">sic mores occalluere,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Col.
					8, 16, 6</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
